\f2\b \AppleTypeServices \cf0 \strokec5 Context Injection and Grounded Generation\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 Context injection
\f1\b0  means placing retrieved text into the prompt so it becomes part of the conditioning sequence. \

\f2\b \strokec5 Grounding
\f1\b0 \strokec5  is the property that every factual claim in the output is supported by the provided evidence. 
\f5\i \strokec5 Injection is the mechanism; grounding is the contract.
\f1\i0 \strokec5  Injection alone does not guarantee grounding, because the model can still ignore, misread, or supplement the context.
\f2\b \strokec5 \
It has 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 Evidence (Relevant), Instruction ("Answer only from sources; say if not found"), Attribution (Cite source IDs per claim),Abstention (n explicit, acceptable "I don't know" path), Verification (Post-generation check: claims 
\f6 \uc0\u8838 
\f1  evidence)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec5 Product Quantization (PQ) Vs Scalar Quantization (SQ) \
\
RAG : \
"RAG, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, is a pattern where at inference time we retrieve evidence relevant to the user's question from an external knowledge store and inject it into the LLM's context, so the answer is grounded in, and attributable to, that evidence.\
It exists because an LLM has two kinds of memory. Its weights are frozen at a training cutoff, lossy for long-tail facts, contain none of our private data, and can't enforce permissions. Its context window is precise but only lasts for one request. Models are much better at reading than remembering, so RAG's job is to find the right text and put it in front of the model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 That addresses four enterprise problems. Freshness: updating knowledge means re-indexing a document, not retraining. Private data: our documents stay in our store. Attribution: every claim can cite a source. And the one people often miss, access control: we filter by the user's permissions at retrieval time, so the model only ever sees what that user is allowed to see.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 "What's the difference between faithfulness and correctness?"
\f1\b0 \strokec5 \
Faithfulness compares the answer with the context; correctness compares it with the truth. A faithful answer built on an outdated source is still wrong.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \

\f2\b 1 . Metadata : \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec5 Chunking creates the searchable units; metadata tells the retrieval system what those units are, where they came from, who can access them, and when they are valid.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 metadata is one of the things that makes retrieval 
\f2\b accurate, secure, filterable, and maintainable
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

