\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec5 One-line definition for interviews
\fs36  : 
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec5 Semantic chunking is a RAG preprocessing technique that uses semantic similarity between neighboring text segments to identify meaningful topic boundaries, producing coherent chunks that improve the quality of retrieval and downstream LLM responses.\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Parent-Child Chunking (Small-to-Big) : \strokec5 Parent-Child Chunking
\f1\b0 \strokec5  is a RAG retrieval strategy where a document is divided into 
\f2\b \strokec5 small child chunks for precise retrieval
\f1\b0 \strokec5 , while each child chunk belongs to a 
\f2\b \strokec5 larger parent chunk that contains broader context
\f1\b0 \strokec5 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This solves a common RAG problem: 
\f2\b \strokec5 small chunks retrieve precisely but may lack context; large chunks provide context but can hurt retrieval precision.
\f9\b0\fs32 \strokec5 \

\f1\fs24 \strokec5 Parent-child chunking separates retrieval granularity from generation context by indexing small child chunks for precise semantic search while using their larger parent chunks to provide sufficient context to the LLM.
\f9\fs28 \cf6 \strokec6 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs32 \cf0 \strokec5 Interview scenario
\fs48 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Interviewer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 "You have a 500-page hotel policy document. Some policies are very detailed. How would you chunk it?"\strokec5 \
A strong answer:\
\strokec5 "I wouldn't immediately choose a fixed token size. First I'd preserve the document hierarchy\'97document, section, subsection and paragraph. I'd create parent chunks around coherent sections and then divide those parents into smaller child chunks based on semantic or structural boundaries. I'd embed the child chunks because they provide more precise retrieval representations, while maintaining a parent ID for each child. At query time, I'd retrieve the top-K child chunks, expand their parent IDs, deduplicate the parents, and optionally rerank the parent candidates. I'd then construct the final context within a token budget and send that to the LLM. Finally, I'd compare this against recursive or semantic chunking using retrieval and answer-quality metrics."\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f10\b\fs40 \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f2\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Proposition chunking
\f1\b0 \strokec5  (the "Dense X Retrieval" line of work) uses an LLM to rewrite text into atomic, self-contained factual statements ("The flight-disruption exception excludes corporate-account bookings."). This gives very high precision, but ingestion is expensive.\outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \

\f2\b \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 2.3 What Goes Wrong: Size and Overlap Failure Modes : \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Chunk too small : 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 The chunk contains too little context to represent a complete idea , The individual chunks don't contain the complete rule.Important information split across chunks
\f2\b \strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Chunk too Large : 
\f1\b0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 A chunk contains too many unrelated concepts,contains lots of irrelevant information , Higher inference cost , More latency , Context-window pressure & Possible distraction of the LLM 
\f2\b \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Overlap too large : 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 The same content is repeated across many neighboring chunks, effectively retrieve 
\f2\b \strokec5 one piece of information many times
\f1\b0 \strokec5 .
\f2\b \strokec5 \
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Overlap too small : 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 Important information at a chunk boundary can be separated.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Chunking exists for three main reasons: embedding models have input limits, one vector must represent the entire chunk, and the LLM has a token budget. The second reason is the most important. If a chunk mixes several topics, its vector lands between them and matches none strongly, so large chunks cause retrieval misses. Small chunks match precisely but lose context: a sentence like 'this exception does not apply to corporate bookings' is useless without knowing which exception.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl120\slmult1\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 So chunk size trades match precision against answer completeness. \
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Overlap protects facts that straddle boundaries: with overlap O, any span up to O+1 tokens is guaranteed intact in some chunk, at a storage cost of S over S minus O, which is why 10\'9620% overlap is a sensible default. \
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Too much overlap multiplies cost and fills top-K with near-duplicates.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sl120\slmult1\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f11 \uc0\u9642 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 My default is structure-aware chunking: split on the document's own structure (headings, clauses, slides, functions), recursively split oversized sections, and prefix each chunk with its document title and heading path so it stands alone. \
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f11 \uc0\u9642 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 For policy and manual content, I use parent-child: embed small children of around 200 tokens for precise matching, but return the parent section to the LLM. I choose per document type: clause-level for legal with cross-reference handling, one Q&A pair per chunk for FAQs, one product per chunk with structured attributes kept as filters for catalogs, AST-based for code, and tables with repeated headers, units, and periods for financial reports.\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f11 \uc0\u9642 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 I never pick a size by convention. I run an experiment on a labeled eval set, compare configurations at an equal context-token budget rather than equal K, since bigger chunks otherwise get unfair extra context, and segment the results by query type. Semantic chunking is something I test against a recursive baseline rather than assume, since its gains are often inconsistent relative to its ingestion cost."\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
