\f1 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec5 The main index families: \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 HNSW is a layered proximity graph with greedy descent and beam search at the bottom layer. Recall is tuned with M, ef_construction, and ef_search at query time. It's the low-latency default but memory-hungry and weak with deletes. \
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 IVF clusters vectors with k-means and scans only the nprobe nearest clusters, which is cheaper in memory and pairs well with product quantization. \
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 PQ splits vectors into sub-vectors and stores one-byte centroid codes, roughly 64\'d7 compression, using table-lookup distances. \
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 IVF-PQ with a full-precision refine step is the classic billion-scale recipe. \
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 In practice I often start with HNSW plus int8 quantization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
