\f2\b\fs32 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 Scenario :
\fs48 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 If an interviewer asks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 "Explain score normalization and weighted sum fusion in RAG."
\f1\b0 \strokec5 \
A strong answer would be:\

\f2\b \strokec5 "Score normalization plus weighted sum is a hybrid retrieval fusion technique used to combine results from multiple retrievers such as BM25 and dense vector search. Since different retrievers produce scores on different scales, we first normalize their scores to a common range, such as 0 to 1. We then assign weights based on the relative importance of each retrieval strategy and calculate a combined score. For example, if BM25 has a normalized score of 0.8 and vector search has 0.9, with weights of 0.4 and 0.6, the final score is 0.4 \'d7 0.8 + 0.6 \'d7 0.9 = 0.86. We repeat this for all candidates, sort by the fused score, and pass the top candidates to an optional reranker and then the LLM. The key advantage is that keyword and semantic retrieval can complement each other, while the main challenge is choosing an appropriate normalization method and tuning the weights."
\f1\b0 \strokec5 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Problem: score distributions vary 
\f5\i \strokec5 per query
\f1\i0 \strokec5 . One outlier BM25 score compresses all other normalized scores toward zero. \uc0\u945  also needs tuning, and the best \u945  may vary by query type.\
\

\f2\b 2. Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) 
\f1\b0 : RRF does not care about the actual retrieval scores. It cares about the rank/position of a document in each retriever's result list.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Forget the scores. Use the rankings. 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 Documents that consistently rank highly across multiple retrieval systems get rewarded.\
Formula : 1/(k+rank).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs32 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 Scenario :
\fs48 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 Interviewer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 "You have BM25 and vector search. BM25 returns scores between 0 and 100, while vector search returns cosine similarity between 0 and 1. How would you combine them?"\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 Strong answer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 "I would consider Reciprocal Rank Fusion if I don't want to depend on score calibration. I would retrieve the top-K documents from both BM25 and vector search, assign each document a rank within each result set, and calculate an RRF score using
\f16 \strokec5  
\f1 \outl0\strokewidth0 1/(k+rank).\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 \
\strokec5 .Documents that rank highly across both retrievers receive a higher fused score. This avoids the problem of comparing incompatible score scales. If I have reliable score normalization and need explicit control over the relative importance of each retriever, I could instead use normalized scores with a weighted sum. I would evaluate both approaches using metrics such as Recall@K, MRR, and NDCG."\strokec5 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 4. Top-K and Retrieval Metrics : \
\
\strokec5 Top-K
\f1\b0 \strokec5  
\f11 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  How many retrieved documents/chunks do we keep  : 
\f2\b \strokec5 Retrieval Metrics
\f1\b0 \strokec5  
\f11 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  How do we measure whether those retrieved documents are actually good .\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Choosing K is a retrieval-quality vs context-noise trade-off.
\f1\b0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Metrics : \

\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Recall@K
\f1\b0  -  measures what proportion of 
\f14\i\fs32 \strokec5 all
\f1\i0\fs24 \strokec5  the possible relevant items in the entire database were successfully captured within the top 
\f10\b\fs32 \strokec5 K
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec5  results\
