\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Retrieval has two complementary families. \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls32\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Dense retrieval uses a bi-encoder: queries and chunks are embedded independently and matched by ANN search. It handles paraphrase and cross-lingual matching, but struggles with exact IDs, rare entities, numbers, and negation. \
\ls32\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Sparse retrieval, typically BM25 over an inverted index, scores exact term overlap with IDF weighting so rare terms matter more, TF saturation so repetition has diminishing returns, and length normalization. It's excellent for codes and names but blind to synonyms.\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 Because they make different errors, I run both in parallel and fuse them. \
My default is Reciprocal Rank Fusion, one over 60 plus rank summed across retrievers, because it's rank-based, needs no score calibration, and rewards documents both retrievers agree on. \
Weighted normalized scores can do better when tuned on good eval data, but they're fragile to per-query score distributions. In practice, the reranker over the union of candidates often acts as the real fusion step.\strokec5 \
\strokec5 I distinguish two Ks. K_retrieve, around 50, is optimized for recall and feeds the reranker; its cost is reranker compute. K_final, typically 3 to 10, goes into the prompt; its cost is LLM tokens, latency, and noise. I make K_final adaptive using reranker score thresholds and the token budget.\strokec5 \
\strokec5 I measure Recall@K for the candidate stage, since that's the ceiling, NDCG and MRR for ranking quality, and Precision@K_final for context noise. Recall saturates as K grows while cost grows linearly, so I choose K from the knee of the recall curve on a golden set."\strokec5 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 7 \'97 QUERY TRANSFORMATION
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 IN RAG  - 
\f9 \cb9 \strokec5 the process of using a Large Language Model (LLM) to modify, rephrase, or break down a user's raw input question before it is sent to a vector database or search engine\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls33\ilvl0
\f1 \cf0 \cb1 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Query Rewriting 
\f1\b0 - turns a query into a clear, standalone, retrieval-friendly form. This includes 
\f2\b \strokec5 conversational condensation
\f1\b0 \strokec5  (resolving "it," "that one," "what about Abu Dhabi?"), spelling correction, and removing noise.\
\ls33\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Query Expansion - 
\f1\b0 \strokec5 adds synonyms, acronyms, and related terms, mostly to help BM25.\
\ls33\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Query Decomposition - 
\f1\b0 splits a complex question into independent sub-queries.\
\ls33\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}HyDE - 
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 the LLM writes a 
\f5\i \strokec5 hypothetical answer passage
\f1\i0 \strokec5 , and you embed and search with 
\f2\b \strokec5 that
\f1\b0 \strokec5  instead of the question. The intuition is that answer-to-answer similarity is higher than question-to-answer similarity.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 Metadata Filtering\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 What it does:
\f1\b0  restricts search to relevant subsets (region, product, version, date, doc type). Filters can come from three sources:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls34\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 UI facets
\f1\b0 , which are the most reliable.\
\ls34\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The user profile
\f1\b0  (their region, their product tier).\
\ls34\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Extraction from the query.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Hard vs soft filters.
\f1\b0  A hard filter excludes non-matching documents. A soft filter 
\f5\i \strokec5 boosts
\f1\i0 \strokec5  matching documents instead. Use hard filters for security and definite constraints ("version 4"), and soft boosts for uncertain extractions.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls35\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\'95	}Interview :\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls35\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Routing decides the backend. I cascade a cheap semantic router or classifier, escalating to an LLM only when confidence is low. Self-query converts language into schema-validated filters. The key safety rule is that LLM-generated filters can only narrow content, never touch security filters, with a fallback that relaxes content filters on zero results.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls36\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 For latency, I combine rewriting, routing, decomposition, expansion, and filter extraction into one structured-output call with a small model, so the whole plan costs about 300 milliseconds.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 PART 9 \'97 RE-RANKING
\f1\b0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
It is 
\f9 \cb9 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 a second-pass evaluation step that re-orders an initial, broad set of retrieved documents so the most contextually relevant information sits at the very top before being fed to the Large Language Model\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \cb1 \strokec5 Retriever 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  find a reasonably large set of potentially relevant documents.
\f1\b0 \strokec5 \

\f2\b \strokec5 Reranker 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f2  carefully determine which of those candidates are actually most relevant to the query.
\f1\b0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\
Cross-Encoder : \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The important thing is that 
\f2\b \strokec5 the query and document can directly interact through Transformer self-attention at the token level
\f1\b0 \strokec5 .\outl0\strokewidth0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Cross-Encoders generally provide better pairwise relevance modeling because query and document tokens interact through the Transformer's attention layers, allowing the model to capture fine-grained semantic relationships, terms, negations, and contextual dependencies. The trade-off is significantly higher inference cost because each query-document pair must be evaluated independently."\outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\
Interview :\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls37\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Vector similarity alone isn't enough because bi-encoders compress the query and document independently into single vectors. There's no token-level interaction, so they capture topical similarity, not whether this passage answers this question, and they blur negation, numbers, and conditions. That's acceptable for the first stage, whose job is recall.\
\ls37\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 So I use two-stage retrieval: hybrid retrieval fetches around 50 candidates, then a cross-encoder reads each query\'96passage pair jointly, with full attention between them, and outputs a relevance score. Cross-encoders can't precompute anything, so they're only affordable on a shortlist. \
\ls37\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 LLM rerankers, especially listwise, give the best quality and can follow instructions like 'prefer the current official policy,' but at roughly 15K input tokens and one to three seconds per query, I reserve them for a cascade on complex or high-value queries.\
\ls37\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 For options, managed APIs like Cohere Rerank give quality with zero ops but send data externally. Open-source rerankers like BGE's multilingual cross-encoder can be self-hosted on GPUs, which matters for data residency.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\
\
\
\
