\fs28 \cf0 \strokec5 Interview Answer (about 2 minutes)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 \'93Before choosing any technology, I\'92d spend the first few minutes on requirements, because in RAG they decide most of the architecture.\strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls48\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b Outcome
\f1\b0  - \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 First, the outcome: what does the user do with the answer, and what does a wrong answer cost? That determines how strict grounding and abstention must be.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Taxonomy
\f1\b0  : \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Second, I\'92d ask for real questions from search logs or tickets and build a query taxonomy. It tells me whether this is even a RAG problem. Aggregations belong in SQL, actions belong in tools, and navigational lookups might just need better search. RAG earns its place on synthesis across documents. The taxonomy also tells me whether I need lexical retrieval for IDs and error codes, which dense embeddings handle unreliably.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b Data : 
\f1\b0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Third, the data: which systems are authoritative, how they change, their formats (scanned PDFs and tables change parsing completely), and their permission models. The index becomes a second copy of the security model, so it must carry ACLs and stay in sync.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b Cost
\f1\b0  : \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Fourth, measurable NFRs: p95 time to first token, availability with a defined degraded mode, residency, a cost-per-query envelope, and two separate freshness SLOs. Content freshness is a quality issue; permission revocation is a security issue.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b Estimation
\f1\b0  : \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Then quick estimates. For 20,000 employees, I\'92d expect only a few QPS at peak, so throughput isn\'92t the binding constraint. LLM tokens per minute, the latency budget, and permissions are.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\b Evaluations
\f1\b0  : \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Finally, I\'92d agree acceptance gates up front: zero cross-permission leaks as a hard gate, recall and faithfulness thresholds per query category on a golden set, and a business KPI measured against a control group.\strokec5 \
\ls48\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Only then would I sketch the architecture, and I\'92d tie every component back to one of these requirements.\'94\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 The business can\'92t give you labeled data. Now what?\'94
\f1\b0 \
I hold short SME sessions where experts write gold answers for about 50 real questions. I add LLM-generated questions from the documents, reviewed by a human. Synthetic questions tend to share words with their source passages, which flatters lexical retrieval, so I weight real questions more. Then the set grows from production feedback, especially thumbs-down answers and escalations.\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
