\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Interview Answer (about 2.5 minutes)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec5 I separate the system into four planes.\strokec5 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls50\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f8 \uc0\u8259 
\f2 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The offline indexing plane
\f1\b0  uses connectors driven by events plus periodic reconciliation, feeding a queue with retries and a dead-letter queue. A document registry tracks the version, content hash, and ACL hash of every document, so permission-only changes update metadata without re-		embedding. Documents go through layout-aware parsing, with OCR only where scans matter, then cleaning and metadata extraction, including effective dates and status so superseded policies can be excluded. Then ACL mapping and PII handling, structure-aware chunking with parent links and 	page anchors, and embedding with the model version recorded on every vector. We write to a vector index, a BM25 index, and filterable metadata fields.\strokec5 \
\ls50\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f8 \uc0\u8259 
\f2 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 The online plane
\f1\b0  authenticates the user and resolves their groups into a permission filter, and if that fails, it fails closed. It rewrites follow-up questions and routes: documents go to RAG, numbers go to SQL or APIs, IDs go to exact lookups. Then it runs dense and sparse retrieval in parallel, with 	the permission filter inside each search, fuses the results with RRF, reranks about 50 candidates with a cross-encoder, and builds a deduplicated, budgeted context with citation IDs. The principle is recall early, precision late.\strokec5 \
\ls50\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f8 \uc0\u8259 
\f2 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Generation
\f1\b0  uses a versioned prompt that separates trusted instructions from untrusted evidence, calls the LLM through a gateway with fallbacks, and validates citations before delivery. High-risk routes add claim-level verification.\strokec5 \
\ls50\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f8 \uc0\u8259 
\f2 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Operations
\f1\b0  wrap all of it: per-stage traces, a golden set that gates CI, sampled online evaluation, index versions switched via aliases, and a degradation ladder down to search-only mode.\strokec5 \
\ls50\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f15 \uc0\u8259 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 That is my baseline. Query expansion, compression, graph retrieval, and agentic loops come in only when evaluation shows a specific failure they fix.\'94\strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 .  
\f2\b Follow Up Questions :
\f1\b0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Your pipeline has a dozen stages. Why not just embed, take the top 5, and generate?\'94
\f1\b0 \
That is my prototype, and it fails exactly where enterprises get hurt:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls51\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 exact IDs (no sparse retrieval),\
\ls51\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 permissions (no ACL filter),\
\ls51\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 stale versions (no metadata),\
\ls51\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 ranking precision (no reranker),\
\ls51\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 debuggability (no traces).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 Most of the added stages are offline and add no query latency. The online additions (parallel sparse retrieval, fusion, reranking) cost a few hundred milliseconds against an LLM that takes seconds. Each one stays only if the golden set shows it earns its place.\

\f2\b Where exactly do you enforce permissions? Why not filter right after retrieval, before the LLM?\'94
\f1\b0 \
Inside the search, as a filter on principals in both the dense and sparse queries. Filtering after retrieval but before generation doesn\'92t leak if it\'92s implemented correctly, but it has two problems:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls52\ilvl0
\f2\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Recall collapses.
\f1\b0  If 45 of the top 50 results are forbidden, the user gets 5 weak candidates.\
\ls52\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 It\'92s fragile.
\f1\b0  Every code path, cache, and future feature must remember to apply the filter, and one omission is a leak.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 	\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 The reranker adds about 250 ms. Justify it.
\f1\b0 \
With evidence. I compare precision@5, NDCG, and end-to-end correctness on the golden set, with and without the reranker. In context, 250 ms is about 5% of a ~5-second request. A better ranking also lets me send fewer chunks, which cuts input tokens and partly pays for itself. If the evaluation shows no gain, I remove it. If it shows a gain but latency is tight, I reduce the candidate count, use a smaller reranker, or batch requests on GPU.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec5 Answers are bad. Which component do you fix first?\'94
\f1\b0 \
None, until I\'92ve localized the failure. I walk a failing sample through the trace, asking at each step:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls53\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Is the answer in the corpus at all?\
\ls53\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Was it parsed correctly?\
\ls53\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Is it in the top 50 candidates?\
\ls53\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Is it in the reranked top 10?\
\ls53\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Did it make it into the final context?\
\ls53\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec5 Was it used faithfully in the answer?\
